## Known problem

The known pattern is explicit ADB target pinning: Android recommends `-s <serial>` when multiple devices exist, and `-s` overrides ambient `$ANDROID_SERIAL`. The trap is trusting a serial’s spelling—or losing the chosen serial between processes—instead of verifying the transport/device identity. [Android ADB documentation](https://developer.android.com/tools/adb), [official ADB man page](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/adb/+/refs/heads/main/docs/user/adb.1.md).

## Axes

1. FOUND: `WISPR_SERIAL` reaches `device(env)`, whose explicit-serial path checks only that it is attached (`scripts/uat/wispr_eyes.py:328-340`); `_switch_to` only tracks debt (`:376-385`); then `is_emulator()` trusts `startswith("emulator-")` (`:1857-1859`). Thus an attached physical device with an emulator-looking serial bypasses `RECORDING_IS_OFF`. A running emulator take cannot normally switch to the phone because its take debt is written under the selected serial (`:1506-1510`) and `_switch_to` refuses outstanding debt. Fix: make `is_emulator` require both the prefix and `adb -s <target> shell getprop ro.kernel.qemu == 1`; add a test where `WISPR_SERIAL=emulator-looking-phone` and `ro.kernel.qemu=0` keeps all five recording guards closed.

2. FOUND: The proposed `ready()` branch’s intended guard is `is_emulator()`, but the prefix-only classification above can therefore send the swipe/PIN/ENTER sequence to that physical device. Today the lock refusal is at `scripts/uat/wispr_eyes.py:419-432`. Fix: use the strengthened identity probe from axis 1 and repeat it inside `unlock_emulator()` immediately before its first input command; on any unclear/false result, raise `Blocked` without sending input.

3. FOUND: The seven §11.2 allow controls are `adb logcat`, `adb shell dumpsys`, `adb install`, `adb shell am instrument`, `adb devices`, `grpcurl … list`, and `wispr_eyes.py look`; add the promised §3 control `adb shell pm list packages`. Harness subprocesses remain invisible because the hook receives only the outer `tool_input.command` (`scripts/hooks/command-safety.py:274-281`). But `$ADB shell input …` is tokenized literally: `executable()` returns basename `$ADB`, not `adb` (`:128-140`), so the planned deny row fails. Fix in `check_raw_device_driving`: `if re.fullmatch(r"\\$(?:ADB|\\{ADB\\})", name): name = "adb"` before parsing ADB options; test both `$ADB` and `${ADB}`.

4. FOUND: Cross-process restoration is not guaranteed. The journal is `~/.cache/wispr-eyes/restore.json` (`scripts/uat/wispr_eyes.py:78`), written by `_owe_locked` (`:199-211`) and read by `_owed` (`:171-175`), but `_restore_locked` restores only `device()`’s scope (`:2232-2238`). A later plain `restore` can select the physical phone and miss the emulator debt. Also §11.2 requires a non-OK mic command to leave “nothing journaled,” creating a crash window after the state changes but before journaling. Fix: require the later restore to select the recorded emulator explicitly (`restore --serial <serial>`), write and read back `("host-mic", previous)` before sending the console command, and retain that debt on every non-OK/exception. Replace the KO test with “debt remains.” The plan must name an authoritative way to obtain `previous`; `hear_on_emulator` currently only sets and checks `OK` (`:1878-1887`).

5. FOUND: Screen recording can remain outside `open_recorder` (`scripts/uat/rail_signals.py:177-188`), and injection can occur inside it, but `restore()` currently runs only once (`:446`) while `open_recorder` consumes the per-process restore allowance on every take (`scripts/uat/wispr_eyes.py:1464-1470`). The second signal will block. There is also a data mismatch: rail produces JSON packets (`rail_signals.py:150-167`), while planned `inject_audio` accepts raw PCM. Fix each iteration’s order to: `restore()` → write raw PCM → `set_host_mic(False)` → start screen recording → `with open_recorder(): inject_audio(pcm)` → end screen recording → final `restore()`. Delete rail’s packet builder so the harness owns packet construction.

6. NOT FOUND: In the listed implementation files, the only deleted-script pointer is the already-enumerated `rail_signals.py:18` documentation reference, and the only `hear_on_emulator` caller is the already-enumerated `say_into_emulator` at `wispr_eyes.py:1923`.

## Verdict

PROCEED-WITH-REVISIONS — strengthen device identity, close the hook variable gap, make host-mic debt genuinely cross-process/crash-safe, and repair the multi-take rail ordering.